
# ── Journey 4: Workplace Basics ─────────────────────────────────────────────
levels.append({'id':31,'journey_id':4,'journey_title':'Workplace Basics','title':'Communicating with Your Manager',
'scenario':'You have a weekly one-on-one with your manager, Sarah. Last week you missed a deadline because you were blocked by a dependency from another team and you did not communicate about it proactively. Sarah has brought it up at the start of the meeting, and you can tell she is not angry but she wants to understand what happened and make sure it does not happen again.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'Sarah opens the one-on-one by saying, "I want to talk about the report that was late last week — can you walk me through what happened?" How do you respond?',
'"Of course. I was blocked waiting on data from the analytics team. I should have flagged it to you by Tuesday when I realised it was at risk instead of waiting until Friday. That\'s on me."',
['"The analytics team didn\'t deliver their data on time — it wasn\'t really my fault."','Say, "It was a really busy week and a lot of things came up."','Say, "I\'m sorry — I didn\'t realise it was going to be a problem until it was too late."'],
'A clear cause, an honest acknowledgment of where you should have acted differently, and ownership of your part in the gap — without placing blame — is exactly what a manager needs to hear.',
'Immediately blaming another team without acknowledging your own role signals low accountability. "Really busy week" is vague and does not explain the specific breakdown. Claiming you did not realise it was a problem until Friday may be true but lacks the self-analysis that builds trust.'),
mcq(2,'Sarah says, "That\'s helpful — what stopped you from flagging it earlier?" How do you answer honestly?',
'"Honestly, I think I was hoping it would resolve itself and I didn\'t want to surface a problem until I was sure it was one. I\'ve learned that\'s the wrong call — the earlier you know, the more options you have."',
['"I didn\'t want to bother you with something that might sort itself out."','"I just forgot — I had a lot on my plate."','"I wasn\'t sure it was worth flagging. The data came the next day anyway."'],
'An honest self-assessment that names the specific thinking error — "hoping it resolves itself" — combined with the lesson you have drawn shows real professional maturity and is far more reassuring to a manager than any excuse.',
'"I didn\'t want to bother you" is a common but flawed reason that misunderstands the manager\'s role. "I forgot" is not an acceptable reason for a deadline miss. "It came the next day anyway" minimises the impact of the delay and dismisses the conversation.'),
mcq(3,'Sarah nods and says, "What would you do differently next time?" How do you answer specifically?',
'"As soon as I know a dependency is at risk — even if I\'m not sure — I\'ll send you a one-line heads up. No waiting to be sure. Just \'Here\'s the situation, here\'s the risk, I\'ll update you.\'"',
['"I\'ll try to be more careful in general."','"I\'ll try to check in with the other team more often."','Say, "I\'ll probably just do the work myself to avoid depending on others."'],
'A specific, actionable behavioural change with an example of what the communication would look like is the strongest possible answer. It tells Sarah exactly what she can expect next time.',
'"Be more careful in general" is not specific and gives Sarah no confidence that anything will change. "Check in more with the other team" addresses a symptom rather than the real gap, which was communication with Sarah. Avoiding all dependencies is neither practical nor desirable.'),
mcq(4,'Sarah says, "Good — I appreciate that. Is there anything you need from me to help with this kind of thing going forward?" How do you use this opening?',
'"Yes — it would help if I had a standing fifteen-minute slot mid-week for exactly these situations. Even if there is nothing to flag, it gives me the opportunity to surface things before they become problems."',
['"No, I\'m fine — I\'ll figure it out."','"Maybe just be more available? Sometimes I struggle to get time with you."','Say, "I\'ll let you know if I need anything" and leave it vague.'],
'Asking for a specific, reasonable structural solution — a standing mid-week check-in — is professional self-advocacy that directly addresses the problem and gives Sarah something concrete to act on.',
'"I\'m fine" after admitting a breakdown that needed more communication is inconsistent. "Be more available" is a slightly accusatory request without specificity. Leaving it vague wastes the explicit offer Sarah just made.'),
mcq(5,'Sarah agrees to the mid-week slot and the meeting ends positively. How do you follow up after the one-on-one?',
'Send Sarah a short note summarising the agreed solution: "Thanks for the conversation today. I\'ve added the Wednesday 10am slot to both our calendars. I\'ll use it proactively going forward."',
['Do nothing — the conversation covered it.','Send a long email rehashing the whole discussion and reiterating your apologies.','Wait to see if Sarah books the slot herself.'],
'A brief, action-focused follow-up that confirms the specific thing agreed — the time slot — and commits to using it proactively shows that you follow through on conversations with your manager.',
'Doing nothing means the agreement may not materialise into a calendar event. A long rehashing email is unnecessary after a positive resolution. Waiting for Sarah to book it puts the onus of your request back on her.')],
'response':resp('You took honest ownership of a deadline miss, explained your thinking error clearly, proposed a specific behavioural change, made a concrete request for support, and followed up with action. Now type or say aloud the one-line mid-week message you would send Sarah on a Wednesday when there is nothing critical to flag — demonstrating you are using the time proactively.',
'Excellent! How you communicate with your manager in difficult moments defines your professional reputation as much as the work itself. Accountability, specificity, and follow-through are what build genuine trust.')})

levels.append({'id':32,'journey_id':4,'journey_title':'Workplace Basics','title':'Collaborating Across Teams',
'scenario':'You are leading a cross-functional project that requires input from three teams: marketing, engineering, and legal. Each team has different priorities, different communication styles, and different definitions of urgency. The project has a firm external deadline and you are starting to feel the tension between the teams pulling in different directions. You need to pull them into alignment.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You call a joint kickoff meeting with representatives from all three teams. How do you open the meeting to set the right tone from the start?',
'"Thanks for being here. We all have different priorities day to day — that\'s real. What I want to do today is find the shared goal that makes this project worth all of our time, and map out exactly what we each need from each other to get there."',
['"Let\'s all get aligned and move quickly — I need deliverables from each of you by end of week."','Explain your own team\'s requirements in detail first to establish the priorities.','Say, "I know everyone is busy so let\'s keep this short" and rush through the agenda.'],
'Acknowledging different priorities honestly, naming the shared goal, and framing the meeting as mutual dependency mapping immediately positions you as a fair facilitator rather than a self-interested project owner.',
'Leading with your deliverable demands sets an adversarial tone. Explaining your team\'s requirements first signals whose perspective is most important. "I know everyone is busy" and rushing signals that the meeting itself is not worth the time.'),
mcq(2,'The engineering lead says, "We\'re already stretched thin — I need to know exactly what\'s required from us so we can scope it properly." How do you respond?',
'"That\'s exactly what we should work out right now. Engineering, can you walk us through what a well-scoped request from us would look like? Let\'s build the format together."',
['"I\'ll send you a spec after the meeting — just block some time for now."','Say, "The requirements are straightforward — it shouldn\'t take much of your team\'s time."','Tell them the requirements are still being defined and you will follow up later.'],
'Turning a challenge into a collaborative definition exercise — letting engineering lead on what scoping looks like — is tactful, inclusive, and produces a better spec.',
'Promising a spec after the meeting without confirming scope now leaves them unable to plan. Calling the requirements "straightforward" without knowing their workload is dismissive. Saying requirements are still being defined when you called the kickoff wastes their time.'),
mcq(3,'Legal raises a concern that their review process takes three weeks and the project timeline only allows two. How do you handle this in front of all three teams?',
'"Okay — let\'s treat that as a design constraint, not a blocker. Legal, if we get you the materials earlier and structure them in the format you need, can we hit two weeks? What would that look like?"',
['"That\'s going to be a problem — can legal streamline their process for this one?"','Say, "We\'ll figure that out offline — let\'s not get into the weeds here."','Ask legal to explain why it takes three weeks in front of the group.'],
'Reframing a constraint as a design problem and immediately inviting legal to co-design the solution within the constraint is collaborative, respectful, and moves the conversation forward.',
'Asking legal to streamline their process in front of everyone can feel dismissive of their professional standards. "We\'ll figure it out offline" is a deferral that leaves the room without a resolution on a critical path item. Asking them to justify their process publicly is confrontational.'),
mcq(4,'Marketing wants weekly email updates; engineering wants a shared project board; legal wants formal written summaries. How do you resolve the three different communication preferences?',
'"Let\'s do a shared project board as the single source of truth — I\'ll keep it updated and send a one-line weekly digest with a link. Legal, I\'ll add formal summaries at each major milestone."',
['"I\'ll give everyone what they ask for — three separate update formats.','Say, "This is getting complicated — let\'s just use email for everything."','Let each team manage their own communication and check in with you when needed.'],
'One central source with two lightweight adaptations — digest for marketing, milestone summaries for legal — balances everyone\'s needs without creating three times the administrative work.',
'Managing three separate formats for the same information is unsustainable and increases the risk of inconsistency. Defaulting to email for everything ignores the fact that engineering already said they want a project board. Letting teams manage their own communication creates silos.'),
mcq(5,'The project is two weeks in and you sense that tension is building between engineering and marketing over timeline expectations. How do you address this proactively?',
'Send a brief, neutral message to both leads individually: "I want to get ahead of any frustration before it surfaces. Can we do a fifteen-minute three-way to make sure we\'re all reading the timeline the same way?"',
['Wait until the tension becomes visible in a group meeting before addressing it.','Send a group message reassuring everyone that everything is on track.','Ask the engineering lead privately what marketing is doing wrong.'],
'Addressing tension between teams proactively and bilaterally — before it becomes a public conflict — is a hallmark of skilled cross-functional leadership.',
'Waiting for tension to surface in a group setting allows it to escalate and become harder to manage. A reassuring group message that does not address the underlying issue is a band-aid. Going to one side to hear their grievance about the other creates partisanship.')],
'response':resp('You facilitated a strong kickoff, turned constraints into collaborative design problems, resolved communication preference differences gracefully, and intervened proactively before tension escalated. Now type or say aloud the fifteen-minute three-way meeting agenda you would send the engineering and marketing leads.',
'Outstanding! Cross-functional collaboration is where good communicators become indispensable. You showed fairness, inventiveness, and proactive relationship management — the exact skills that move projects forward and build your professional reputation across the organisation.')})

levels.append({'id':33,'journey_id':4,'journey_title':'Workplace Basics','title':'Writing Professional Emails',
'scenario':'You need to write three brief professional emails today: one to a client asking for an overdue payment, one to a colleague requesting help on a tight deadline, and one to your manager asking for time off during a busy period. Each requires a different tone and structure. You want to nail all three.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You are writing the overdue payment email to a client. Which is the best subject line and opening?',
'Subject: "Invoice #4821 — Payment Due" | Opening: "I wanted to follow up on invoice #4821 dated April 3rd, now 14 days past due. I would appreciate payment or a quick note on timing."',
['Subject: "Just Checking In" | Opening: "Hi there! Hope you\'re well. I just wanted to touch base about the invoice..."','Subject: "OUTSTANDING PAYMENT — URGENT" | Opening: "Your invoice is overdue. Please remit immediately."','Subject: "A Small Matter" | Opening: "I feel terrible bringing this up, but I was wondering if the invoice might have been overlooked..."'],
'A clear subject line with the invoice number and status, combined with specific dates and a calm request for payment or communication, is professional, unambiguous, and easy to act on.',
'A vague subject and warm small talk delays the point and can seem evasive. An all-caps, urgent tone is unnecessarily aggressive for a first follow-up. An apologetic subject line undermines your position and makes a normal business request feel like an imposition.'),
mcq(2,'You are writing the help request to your colleague Priya. Which approach works best?',
'"Priya — quick one. I\'m up against a tight deadline Thursday on the market analysis and I\'m stuck on the competitive landscape section. Would you have 30 minutes Wednesday afternoon to look at it together? Happy to return the favour."',
['"Hi Priya, I\'m really struggling with this report and I\'m not sure I\'m going to make the deadline without help — could you help me? I know you\'re busy..."','Send a message saying "Do you have time this week?" without context.','Send Priya the full report with a message: "Can you check this? Due Thursday."'],
'A specific ask — 30 minutes Wednesday, specific section — combined with a clear reciprocal offer is respectful of Priya\'s time and gives her enough context to say yes confidently.',
'An overly apologetic, self-flagellating request with no specifics puts the emotional weight on Priya and makes her feel she is rescuing you. A vague "do you have time" is a context-free request she cannot easily respond to. Sending the full document with a one-liner puts all the work on her.'),
mcq(3,'You are writing the time-off request to your manager. Which approach is best?',
'"Sarah — I\'d like to request Friday May 2nd off for a personal commitment. I\'ve flagged it on the team calendar and checked there are no critical deliverables that day. Let me know if that\'s okay."',
['"I know it\'s a busy period, but is there any chance I might be able to potentially take a day off at some point soon?"','Message Sarah to ask if it is okay to take time off without specifying dates.','Ask for the day off at the end of a long email about an unrelated topic.'],
'A specific date, brief reason, evidence that you have already checked for conflicts, and a clear request for approval is professional self-advocacy done right.',
'"Is there any chance I might potentially take a day off at some point soon" is so hedged it is almost impossible to approve. Asking without specifying dates gives Sarah nothing to act on. Burying a time-off request in an unrelated email makes it easy to miss and signals low confidence.'),
mcq(4,'Priya replies to your help request with, "Sure — but can you send me what you have so far first?" How do you respond?',
'"Of course — sending it now. The main gap is in the competitor benchmarking table. I\'ve flagged it in yellow so you can see exactly where I\'m stuck."',
['"Great! I\'ll send it when I get a chance." (without a timeline)','Send the full report with no further direction, expecting Priya to figure it out.','Reply: "Actually, maybe never mind — I might try to do it myself."'],
'Responding immediately, providing the specific context Priya needs to find the gap quickly — the yellow flag — makes her task easy and shows respect for her time.',
'"I\'ll send it when I get a chance" is ambiguous and unhelpful after someone just agreed to help you. Sending it without direction wastes her time. Backing out after she agreed is inconsiderate and damages trust.'),
mcq(5,'After sending all three emails, you receive no response to the client payment request after 24 hours. How do you follow up?',
'Send a brief, calm follow-up: "Following up on my email from yesterday regarding invoice #4821. If there is an issue I can help resolve, please let me know — otherwise, payment by Friday would be appreciated."',
['Call the client immediately and express your frustration.','Send a stern final notice stating you will escalate to legal.','Wait another week and hope they respond.'],
'A single calm follow-up after 24 hours that opens the door to a problem being the reason — rather than assuming bad faith — is professional and keeps the relationship intact.',
'Calling to express frustration is aggressive after a single missed day. A legal threat is a significant escalation after one non-response. Waiting another week with no follow-up allows the situation to drift further.')],
'response':resp('You wrote three distinct professional emails with appropriate tone, specificity, and structure for each context — an overdue payment follow-up, a peer help request, and a manager time-off request. Now type or say aloud the one-paragraph reply you would send the client if they wrote back saying, "So sorry — completely slipped through. Paying today."',
'Superb! Professional email writing is a daily professional skill that compounds over time. Clear subject lines, specific asks, and appropriate tone for each relationship are what make your written communication easy to act on and a pleasure to receive.')})

levels.append({'id':34,'journey_id':4,'journey_title':'Workplace Basics','title':'Running Effective Meetings',
'scenario':'You have been asked to chair the monthly department meeting. In the past, these meetings have run over time, covered items that could have been handled by email, and often ended without clear actions. This time you want to run a tight, purposeful meeting that respects everyone\'s time and produces real outcomes.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'Before the meeting, you need to build an agenda. What is the most important principle to apply?',
'For each agenda item, specify the type of discussion it requires (decision, update, or discussion) and allocate a time limit to each.',
['List every topic that anyone might want to raise and leave the timing flexible.','Keep the agenda secret until the meeting so people don\'t prepare counterarguments.','Only put items on the agenda that you know will be uncontroversial.'],
'Labelling items by type — decision, update, discussion — focuses the conversation and signals what each item requires from attendees. Time limits create accountability for the facilitator.',
'A long, untimed agenda reliably leads to time overruns and unfinished business. Sharing the agenda in advance gives people the chance to prepare and contribute meaningfully. Avoiding controversial items may keep the peace but defeats the purpose of having a meeting at all.'),
mcq(2,'You open the meeting. How do you start in a way that sets the right expectations?',
'"We have sixty minutes and four items — I\'ll flag when we\'re at the midpoint. If a conversation needs more time than we\'ve allocated, let\'s decide together whether to continue or take it offline. Sound good?"',
['"Let\'s try to keep this short — I know everyone is busy."','Go through the agenda items in order without any setup.','Say, "Who would like to kick us off today?"'],
'Setting the time structure, the midpoint flag, and the explicit group agreement on how to handle over-runs at the start is the single best thing a meeting chair can do.',
'"Let\'s keep it short" is not a structure — it is a vague aspiration. Starting without a setup leaves the room without shared expectations. Asking "who wants to kick off" without a clear process creates a moment of awkward silence.'),
mcq(3,'Agenda item two is a decision: should you approve Budget Option A or B? The discussion has been going for ten minutes longer than planned and you are still in debate. How do you intervene?',
'"We\'re over time on this item. Let me try to summarise where we are: most of us lean toward Option A with concerns about X. Can we make a decision now or do we need one more piece of information? What\'s the blocker?"',
['Let the discussion continue and cut a later item to compensate.','Call a vote immediately without summarising the discussion.','Say, "Let\'s table this for next month" and move on.'],
'A facilitator who summarises the state of the discussion, names the lean, and asks explicitly what is blocking a decision is actively managing toward closure rather than letting the meeting drift.',
'Cutting a later item is a common meeting management failure that frustrates people who prepared for that item. A vote without a summary can produce a decision that some people do not feel heard on. Tabling for next month means the decision takes another month, which may not be acceptable.'),
mcq(4,'A colleague, Martin, keeps raising new points every time you near a decision. How do you manage this without humiliating him?',
'"Martin — I want to make sure we capture that. Can you write it down and we\'ll add it to the parking lot? We\'ll revisit it at the end if we have time, or I\'ll schedule a follow-up for it."',
['Ignore Martin\'s points and proceed to a decision.','Ask the group to vote on whether to keep hearing from Martin.','Say, "Martin — we need to move on" and firmly redirect the agenda.'],
'A parking lot — a visible list of items to revisit — is the professional facilitator\'s best tool for managing a point-raiser without dismissing their contribution or their dignity.',
'Ignoring Martin creates visible frustration and may produce a decision he actively resists. Putting it to a group vote is a public rejection of Martin\'s voice. A firm "we need to move on" works but can feel abrupt and slightly shaming in a group setting.'),
mcq(5,'With five minutes left, you have covered all four items. How do you close the meeting?',
'"Great — let me read back the actions before we go: [Name] will do [X] by [date], [Name] will do [Y] by [date]. I\'ll send a summary within the hour. Thanks everyone — we\'re done five minutes early."',
['"Okay — same time next month. Good meeting everyone."','Ask if anyone has anything else to add before you close.','Send the meeting summary three days later once you have had time to write it carefully.'],
'Closing with a verbal read-back of specific named actions and dates, a commitment to a same-day summary, and using the remaining time to end early is meeting management at its best.',
'"Same time next month, good meeting" gives no accountability for actions and no shared record of decisions. Asking for any other business with five minutes left risks opening a new thirty-minute topic. A summary sent three days later has already lost its relevance to half the participants.')],
'response':resp('You built a purposeful agenda, opened with clear expectations, intervened to close a long debate, managed a persistent contributor with dignity, and closed with action accountability and an early end. Now type or say aloud the meeting summary email you would send within the hour of the meeting ending.',
'Brilliant! Running an effective meeting is one of the most visible professional skills there is. Preparation, facilitation, and follow-through are what transform a meeting from a time cost into a genuine decision-making tool. You showed all three.')})

levels.append({'id':35,'journey_id':4,'journey_title':'Workplace Basics','title':'Giving Updates and Reports',
'scenario':'It is the end of the quarter and your director has asked all team leads to give a five-minute verbal status update on their projects in the leadership meeting. You manage a product development project that is 70% complete, is two weeks behind schedule, and is over budget by 8%. You need to present this in a way that is honest, clear, and solution-focused.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You open your five-minute update. How do you structure the first thirty seconds?',
'"Project X is 70% complete. We\'re two weeks behind the original schedule and 8% over the approved budget. I want to explain why, and then share what we\'re doing about it."',
['"Project X is going really well — we\'ve made strong progress and the team has worked incredibly hard."','"We\'ve been facing some challenges on Project X but we\'re working hard to resolve them."','"I wanted to first give some context about the complexity of this project before getting to the current status."'],
'Leading with the factual status — progress, schedule, budget — and then committing to explain the why and the plan is exactly what a leadership meeting requires: clarity before context.',
'Opening with "going really well" and "worked incredibly hard" before disclosing a delay and overrun is a misleading frame. "Some challenges but working hard" is vague and defensive. Starting with context before the status makes the audience wait too long for the bottom line.'),
mcq(2,'You are explaining the reason for the two-week delay. Which is the best version?',
'"The delay stems from two causes: a vendor integration took three weeks longer than contracted, and we discovered a security requirement in week six that required redesign. Both were addressable but cost us time."',
['"The delay happened due to various factors outside our control."','Apologise extensively before explaining: "I\'m really sorry about this — I take full responsibility for everything."','"The team was not aware of certain requirements early enough, which created some delays."'],
'Two specific, named causes with a brief note that both were addressable demonstrates command of the situation. It gives leadership exactly the information they need to assess the risk going forward.',
'"Various factors outside our control" is the classic non-explanation that leadership immediately discounts. An extensive apology before the explanation delays the information and sounds defensive. The passive "team was not aware" implies poor management and does not name the specific cause.'),
mcq(3,'A director asks, "What is the plan to recover the two weeks?" How do you answer?',
'"We\'re running two workstreams in parallel for the first time on this project — QA and front-end development — which we conservatively estimate recovers one of the two weeks. The second week we\'re treating as a real deadline shift and have updated stakeholders accordingly."',
['"We\'re working as fast as we can to catch up."','Say, "I believe we can still make the original date if everything goes to plan."','Say, "We\'re exploring options and will have a plan by end of week."'],
'A specific recovery mechanism — parallel workstreams — with a realistic recovery estimate — one of two weeks — and honesty about the remaining shift is exactly the level of precision a director needs.',
'"Working as fast as we can" is not a plan. "I believe we can make it if everything goes to plan" is both unrealistic and dishonest about the risk. Saying you will have a plan by end of week when they are asking now signals you are not prepared.'),
mcq(4,'A director asks, "What caused the 8% budget overrun?" How do you answer clearly and without defensiveness?',
'"The overrun is almost entirely attributable to the unplanned security redesign — we had to bring in an external specialist for three weeks at short notice. The hourly rate was not in the original scope."',
['"The budget was always a bit tight — we flagged that at the start."','"There were some costs we didn\'t anticipate — it\'s hard to predict everything in a project like this."','Pivot quickly: "The overrun is manageable — let\'s focus on the path forward."'],
'A specific, bounded explanation — one cause, one external specialist, three weeks, short notice — is precise and gives leadership a clear picture of a non-recurring cost driver.',
'"The budget was always tight" sounds like you are pre-positioning blame on whoever set the budget. "Hard to predict" is defensively vague. Pivoting away from the question before fully answering it can feel evasive, especially to a financial director.'),
mcq(5,'Your update is over. The meeting chair says, "Thanks — anything else from your side?" How do you close?',
'"One thing: to prevent this in future, I\'m adding a requirement review gate at week four of all new projects. I\'ll share the template with the other team leads in case it\'s useful."',
['"No — that\'s everything, thanks."','Apologise again for the delay and overrun before sitting down.','Ask the directors if they have any other questions for you.'],
'Closing with a proactive, preventive measure — the week four gate — and offering to share it signals that you have learned, are improving the system, and are generous with what you have learned.',
'A plain "no that\'s everything" is fine but forgettable. A second apology after giving a thorough and professional update is unnecessary and slightly undermines the professionalism of everything that came before. Asking if there are more questions is appropriate but weaker than leaving with a constructive close.')],
'response':resp('You opened with factual clarity, explained both causes specifically, gave a realistic recovery plan, answered the budget question precisely, and closed with a proactive systemic improvement. Now type or say aloud the one-page written project status report you would send to your director following the verbal update.',
'Outstanding! Giving honest, structured, solution-oriented updates in leadership forums is what builds credibility and trust at every level of an organisation. You showed what professional accountability actually looks like.')})

levels.append({'id':36,'journey_id':4,'journey_title':'Workplace Basics','title':'Managing Up',
'scenario':'Your manager, David, is extremely busy and often gives you feedback that is too high-level to act on, or gives it so late that it is hard to incorporate. He means well but the communication pattern is creating real inefficiency for you. You want to manage up — reshaping how David communicates with you — in a way that makes both your lives easier without it feeling like criticism.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You have a one-on-one with David coming up. You want to raise the feedback pattern issue. How do you open the conversation?',
'"David, I want to talk about something that I think could make our collaboration more efficient — specifically how we handle feedback on deliverables. Is now a good time?"',
['"David, I feel like I never get useful feedback from you on time."','Bring up specific past instances where his feedback was late or too vague.','Wait to see if David raises it himself before you say anything.'],
'Framing the issue as an efficiency opportunity and asking for permission to enter the topic is both collegial and respectful — you are proposing a collaboration improvement, not making a complaint.',
'Leading with "I feel like I never get useful feedback" is accusatory and immediately puts David on the defensive. Bringing up specific past instances as evidence can feel like a grievance list. Waiting indefinitely for David to raise it means the pattern continues.'),
mcq(2,'David says, "Sure — what\'s on your mind?" How do you describe the pattern without blaming him?',
'"When I receive feedback close to a deadline or in general terms, it is hard for me to incorporate it meaningfully. I\'d love to experiment with getting your initial reaction at an earlier stage — even just a few notes — so I can course-correct before the final draft."',
['"Sometimes your feedback comes really late and it\'s too high-level to use."','"I sometimes feel like you don\'t really have time to properly review my work."','"I\'ve noticed a pattern where the feedback loop could work better — but maybe it\'s just me.'],
'Describing the impact on your own workflow — hard to incorporate — rather than his behaviour, and immediately proposing a concrete solution — earlier stage review — is respectful and practical.',
'"Feedback comes really late and is too high-level" directly characterises his behaviour as flawed. "Don\'t have time to properly review" implies neglect. "Maybe it\'s just me" undermines the legitimate point you are making.'),
mcq(3,'David says, "That\'s fair — I do get pulled in many directions. What are you suggesting exactly?" How do you propose the change?',
'"What if I send you a short summary and the two or three specific questions I need your input on at the 50% mark? It should take you five minutes and it would save both of us a lot of last-minute scrambling."',
['"Could you just make sure you review things earlier from now on?"','"Maybe we could have a weekly feedback meeting?"','"Whatever works best for you — I\'ll adapt to your schedule.'],
'A specific, low-effort proposal — a structured email with targeted questions at the halfway mark — makes it easy for David to say yes and gives him the structure he clearly needs to give useful feedback.',
'"Make sure you review earlier" puts the entire burden on David without a mechanism. A weekly feedback meeting adds a recurring calendar commitment to a very busy person. "Whatever works best for you" is helpful in spirit but leaves the problem unsolved.'),
mcq(4,'David agrees and says, "That sounds good — let\'s try it." Two weeks later you send the 50% summary and receive no response. How do you follow up?',
'Send a gentle nudge: "Hi David — circling back on the summary I sent Tuesday. The draft is due Friday — even a quick \'looks good\' or the top one or two concerns would be incredibly helpful."',
['Do nothing and assume David approved it by not responding.','Resend the email with "URGENT — NEEDS RESPONSE" in the subject.','Go directly to David\'s manager to flag the communication gap.'],
'A brief, friendly nudge that restates the timeline and lowers the bar for his response — "even a quick looks good" — makes it easy for a busy person to action without feeling badgered.',
'Assuming silence is approval risks submitting work David may have concerns about. "URGENT — NEEDS RESPONSE" escalates a normal follow-up into an anxious alarm. Going to his manager is a significant escalation after a single missed email.'),
mcq(5,'After three months, the 50% summary approach is working well. How do you acknowledge this with David in a way that reinforces the behaviour?',
'In your one-on-one, say: "I wanted to thank you for the early-stage reviews — they\'ve genuinely improved the quality of my final submissions and saved me a lot of revision time. It\'s working really well."',
['Say nothing — you don\'t want to make it a bigger deal than it is.','Tell other colleagues how much better things are with David now.','Send David a thank-you gift as a sign of appreciation.'],
'Explicitly naming the improvement and thanking David for his role in it in a private professional context reinforces the behaviour and builds the relationship.',
'Saying nothing when something is working removes the positive reinforcement that makes it continue. Telling other colleagues about it can feel like gossip. A gift for a communication pattern change is an unusual response that may make David uncomfortable.')],
'response':resp('You raised a sensitive pattern with your manager diplomatically, proposed a specific low-effort solution, followed up calmly when it was not enacted, and reinforced the behaviour when it worked. Now type or say aloud the 50% summary template you would use — the format of the structured email you send David at the midpoint of a project.',
'Excellent! Managing up is one of the most sophisticated professional communication skills there is. You made David\'s life easier and yours more effective at the same time — and you did it without a single accusation or complaint.')})

levels.append({'id':37,'journey_id':4,'journey_title':'Workplace Basics','title':'Navigating Office Politics',
'scenario':'You have just discovered that two senior colleagues, Helena and Marcus, are in a long-standing rivalry. Both have tried to recruit you to their side on a particular decision about your department\'s new strategy. You like both of them personally and you want to stay neutral, maintain your integrity, and avoid becoming a pawn in their conflict.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'Helena approaches you in the corridor and says, "Between us — I know Marcus\'s approach is going to be a disaster. I hope you\'re seeing through it." How do you respond?',
'"I\'ve heard both sides and I\'m still working through my own view. I don\'t want to get pulled into the middle of a broader disagreement — I hope you understand."',
['"Oh absolutely — I completely agree with you, Helena."','"Marcus actually made some good points too — let me tell you what he said."','Say nothing and nod noncommittally, leaving Helena to assume you agree.'],
'Naming neutrality clearly and respectfully — without revealing what Marcus said — is honest, principled, and closes the door on the recruitment attempt without being impolite.',
'Agreeing with Helena locks you into her camp and commits you to a position that may not be yours. Sharing what Marcus told you in private is a serious breach of trust. Nodding noncommittally lets Helena believe you agree, which she will almost certainly act on.'),
mcq(2,'The next day Marcus approaches you and says, "I hear Helena has been bending your ear. Don\'t let her manipulate you." How do you respond?',
'"I appreciate the heads up — and for what it\'s worth, I\'m trying to stay out of the wider conflict and evaluate the strategy on its merits. I\'m not going to take sides."',
['"Yes, she did speak to me — she said you\'re going to cause a disaster."','"I haven\'t spoken to anyone, Marcus." (lying)','Say, "It\'s fine — I can handle myself" and walk away.'],
'Acknowledging his concern without betraying what Helena said, clearly restating your neutrality, and explaining your evaluation criteria is principled and consistent.',
'Repeating Helena\'s words to Marcus is a serious betrayal of confidence that will destroy trust with both of them. Lying about speaking to Helena is an integrity failure that can unravel quickly. Deflecting without engagement fails to close the recruitment attempt.'),
mcq(3,'You are in a department meeting and Helena and Marcus are starting to direct pointed questions at each other through you as an intermediary. How do you respond?',
'"I\'d like to weigh in on the substance of the strategy itself rather than the debate between options. My view is [your actual position, based on merit]. I\'ll let you both take it from there."',
['Answer Helena\'s pointed question in a way that implicitly supports Marcus.','Stay completely silent to avoid being seen as taking either side.','Ask to take a five-minute break and allow them to speak directly to each other.'],
'Asserting your own substantive position — based on merit — removes you from the role of intermediary and establishes your credibility as an independent voice in the room.',
'Implicitly supporting one side while seeming neutral is dishonest and will eventually be noticed. Staying completely silent makes you look passive and unengaged. Calling a break to get them to talk directly can work but does not address the fact that you are being used as an intermediary.'),
mcq(4,'After the meeting, a third colleague tells you, "You know what this is really about — Helena and Marcus had a falling out two years ago. You\'re being used." How do you respond?',
'"I appreciate you telling me. I\'ve already tried to make my neutrality clear to both of them. I\'m just going to stay focused on what I actually think is the right call for the department."',
['"Tell me everything — I need to know the background to protect myself."','"I suspected as much. Do you think I should talk to our director about it?"','Say, "Wow — office politics are so toxic" and vent your frustration to them.'],
'Thanking them for the information, confirming your approach, and staying focused on the work is composed and professional.',
'Asking for all the background gossip pulls you further into the dynamic you are trying to stay clear of. Escalating to the director after one conversation about a personal history conflict is premature. Venting to a third colleague about office politics is unprofessional and can spread.'),
mcq(5,'In the end, the department makes a decision that aligns more with Marcus\'s approach. Helena is visibly disappointed. How do you handle the aftermath?',
'Approach Helena calmly: "I know that wasn\'t the outcome you were hoping for. I thought your framing of X was really strong — I hope that part carries into the implementation."',
['"Don\'t worry Helena — I actually agreed with you all along."','Avoid Helena for a week until the disappointment blows over.','Go to Marcus and congratulate him effusively to celebrate the outcome.'],
'Approaching the disappointed person with empathy, acknowledging a genuine strength in their position, and connecting it to the future is gracious and maintains the relationship.',
'"I agreed with you all along" is a lie that, if true, means you were dishonest during the whole process. Avoiding Helena signals that you are only interested in winners. Loudly celebrating with Marcus in front of Helena is insensitive and looks like you were in his camp all along.')],
'response':resp('You maintained clear neutrality under sustained recruitment pressure from both sides, established your own substantive position based on merit, stayed above the gossip, and closed the episode graciously with the disappointed party. Now type or say aloud the message you would send Helena two weeks later when you work together on implementing the new strategy.',
'Superb! Navigating office politics with integrity requires more than just staying neutral — it requires actively and consistently naming your position, managing both relationships, and having the courage to engage on the substance rather than the sides. You did all of that.')})

levels.append({'id':38,'journey_id':4,'journey_title':'Workplace Basics','title':'Receiving Constructive Feedback',
'scenario':'During your quarterly review, your manager gives you specific feedback that you sometimes dominate group discussions and do not leave enough space for quieter team members to contribute. This is not what you expected to hear and your first reaction is defensiveness. But you have committed to growing professionally and you know how to receive feedback well.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'Your manager has just delivered the feedback. What is your immediate response?',
'Take a breath, maintain eye contact, and say: "Thank you for telling me — that\'s not easy to hear but I want to understand it better. Can you give me a specific example?"',
['Immediately defend yourself: "I don\'t think that\'s accurate — I always try to include everyone."','Say, "Okay, I\'ll try to talk less" and avoid eye contact.','Ask who told them this: "Was it someone specific who raised this?"'],
'Thanking the giver, acknowledging the discomfort without denial, and asking for a specific example are the three pillars of receiving feedback professionally.',
'Immediately defending yourself shuts down the conversation and signals that feedback is not safe to give you. "I\'ll try to talk less" is reactive and slightly passive-aggressive. Asking who raised it is deflecting — whether it was one person or ten, the feedback deserves engagement.'),
mcq(2,'Your manager gives you a specific example: in last Tuesday\'s brainstorm, you responded to each idea immediately with your own view, which seemed to prevent others from building on each other\'s ideas. How do you respond?',
'"I see exactly what you mean. I was enthusiastic about the topic but I can see how responding to every idea right away might have closed the space before others had a chance to step in. That\'s a useful picture."',
['"I only did that because some of the ideas weren\'t very developed — I was trying to help."','"I don\'t remember it that way — I thought everyone had a chance to speak."','"That\'s fair — I probably should have just stayed quiet.'],
'Reflecting the example back accurately and naming the specific mechanism — responding immediately closes the space — shows that you have genuinely heard the feedback and are thinking about it.',
'Explaining that others\' ideas were underdeveloped comes across as dismissive of their contributions. Questioning whether you remember it that way is an implicit rejection of the feedback. "I should have just stayed quiet" is a vague overcorrection.'),
mcq(3,'Your manager asks, "What do you think you might do differently?" How do you answer?',
'"I could try waiting a full beat before responding to others\' ideas — even if I have a thought ready. And I could be more deliberate about checking in with the quieter people by name: \'Sophie, what do you think?\'"',
['"I\'ll try to talk less overall."','Say, "I\'m not sure — what do you recommend?"','Say, "I\'ll pay attention and see what happens."'],
'Two specific, observable behavioural changes — waiting a beat before responding and naming quieter people by name — show genuine engagement with the feedback and a practical plan.',
'"Talk less overall" is a blunt overcorrection that does not address the specific pattern. Asking your manager what you should do hands responsibility back to them rather than demonstrating ownership. "Pay attention and see what happens" is not a behaviour change.'),
mcq(4,'Your manager says, "That sounds great — and I want to say, the reason I\'m raising this is that you\'re one of the most valuable voices in those rooms. I just want more voices in there with you." How do you receive this?',
'"That\'s genuinely encouraging to hear — and it makes the feedback land differently. I don\'t want to be the ceiling in those conversations; I want to be someone who raises the ceiling for everyone."',
['"Thanks — I suppose I do have a lot of good ideas to share."','"That\'s kind of you to say, but I\'m sure others contribute plenty."','Say nothing and nod, feeling slightly unsure how to receive a compliment.'],
'Receiving the compliment graciously and reframing it in terms of your own aspiration — raising the ceiling for everyone — shows emotional maturity and makes the feedback conversation feel complete and affirming.',
'"I suppose I do have a lot of good ideas" validates the criticism in a slightly self-congratulatory way. Dismissing the compliment ("others contribute plenty") is false modesty. Saying nothing and nodding leaves the conversation on an awkward beat.'),
mcq(5,'After the meeting, you want to follow up to show you are taking the feedback seriously. What do you do?',
'Send your manager a brief message: "I wanted to let you know I\'ve been thinking about our conversation. I\'m going to be deliberate about the two things I mentioned — the pause and calling on quieter people by name. I\'ll check in with you on how it\'s going in a few weeks."',
['Do nothing — the conversation covered it and you\'ll just work on it quietly.','Email each of your quieter team members to apologise for dominating meetings.','Ask your manager to observe your next three meetings to give you a score.'],
'A brief follow-up that names the specific behaviours you are trying and commits to a check-in shows that you are taking the feedback seriously without overreacting.',
'Doing nothing can make the manager wonder whether the conversation landed. Emailing quieter colleagues to apologise makes your personal development process about them, which can be awkward. Asking for a score creates an anxious evaluative dynamic rather than natural development.')],
'response':resp('You received a challenging piece of feedback without defensiveness, engaged with a specific example, proposed two concrete behavioural changes, received the affirmation graciously, and followed up with accountability. Now type or say aloud the check-in message you would send your manager three weeks later with a brief report on how the changes are going.',
'Outstanding! Receiving feedback well is one of the rarest and most admired professional qualities there is. The way you handled this — with curiosity instead of defensiveness, specificity instead of vagueness, and follow-through instead of forgetfulness — is exactly what professional growth looks like.')})

levels.append({'id':39,'journey_id':4,'journey_title':'Workplace Basics','title':'Giving Constructive Feedback',
'scenario':'A junior colleague, Alex, produces reports that have excellent analysis but are consistently submitted late and with formatting errors that require correction before they can be shared. Alex clearly has strong potential and you want to help them improve without demoralising them. You are their informal mentor and you have a regular coffee catch-up today.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You want to raise the lateness and formatting issues. How do you open the conversation?',
'"Alex, there\'s something I\'ve been wanting to share with you — it\'s about your reports. The analytical work in them is genuinely strong, and I want to make sure nothing gets in the way of people seeing that. Is now okay to talk about it?"',
['"Alex, your reports keep being late and the formatting is always off — it\'s becoming a problem."','"I have some feedback about your work — and it\'s not all good."','Wait until Alex asks you directly for feedback before raising it.'],
'Leading with the strength, framing the feedback as protecting their credibility rather than criticising their behaviour, and seeking permission to proceed is supportive and strategic.',
'"Late and the formatting is always off — it\'s becoming a problem" is true but blunt in a way that is likely to make Alex feel ashamed before they have had a chance to respond. "Not all good" creates dread. Waiting until they ask could mean the pattern continues for months.'),
mcq(2,'Alex says, "Of course — what\'s up?" How do you give the specific feedback clearly?',
'"I\'ve noticed two patterns: the reports tend to arrive after the agreed deadline, and there are usually some formatting inconsistencies — section headings, font sizes — that need cleaning up before they go out. I wanted to name them directly because they\'re both fixable."',
['"Basically the reports need to be on time and look more professional."','"To be honest, people have been noticing the late submissions."','"There are just a few small things — nothing major.'],
'Naming two specific, observable patterns — lateness and formatting details — and framing them as fixable is clear, specific, and non-catastrophising.',
'"Need to be on time and look more professional" is vague and slightly condescending. "People have been noticing" is an appeal to anonymous social pressure that can feel threatening. "Nothing major" immediately undermines the seriousness of the feedback you are about to give.'),
mcq(3,'Alex looks slightly crestfallen and says, "I didn\'t realise it was that noticeable." How do you respond?',
'"It\'s noticeable but very addressable — and I\'m raising it now so it doesn\'t become a bigger thing. The quality of your thinking deserves to show up in the full package."',
['Reassure them that it\'s really not that bad after all to make them feel better.','Say, "Yes — a few people have mentioned it to me specifically."','Pivot immediately to solutions without acknowledging how they feel.'],
'Holding the seriousness of the feedback while immediately connecting it to their potential and framing it as an act of care is the balance between honesty and empathy.',
'Walking back the seriousness now creates confusion about how big the issue actually is. Naming who has mentioned it can feel like a betrayal of confidence and adds shame without value. Moving straight to solutions before acknowledging their emotional response can feel cold.'),
mcq(4,'Alex asks, "What can I do to improve?" How do you give them specific, actionable guidance?',
'"For timing: block thirty minutes the morning of the deadline as buffer — not for writing, just for final checks. For formatting: I\'ll share our team style guide. Keep it open in the same window when you\'re finishing the report."',
['"Just be more careful and give yourself more time."','"Read over it once before you send it — that usually catches things."','List twelve specific formatting rules for Alex to memorise.'],
'Two specific, simple, behavioural changes — a morning buffer block and an open style guide — are immediately actionable without being overwhelming.',
'"Be more careful and give yourself more time" is the kind of advice that sounds helpful but gives no new mechanism for change. "Read over it once" is fine advice but vague about what to look for. Listing twelve rules at once is overwhelming and less likely to produce change than two simple habits.'),
mcq(5,'Alex thanks you and says, "I\'m really glad you told me — I want to take this seriously." How do you close the feedback conversation?',
'"I\'m glad. Let\'s check in in three weeks and see how it\'s going. You\'re doing strong work, Alex — this is about making sure everything around the work matches the quality inside it."',
['"Good — I\'ll be keeping an eye on the next few reports."','"Great — and if it keeps happening after this, I\'ll have to escalate it."','Say nothing and close the conversation quickly since it ended positively.'],
'Setting a check-in date, reinforcing the underlying strength, and giving the feedback its purpose — protecting the quality they already have — ends the conversation with motivation and warmth.',
'"I\'ll be keeping an eye on the next few reports" sounds surveillant rather than supportive. Mentioning escalation at the end of a positive, voluntary feedback conversation is unnecessarily ominous. Saying nothing and closing quickly leaves Alex without a forward structure.')],
'response':resp('You gave specific, behavioural, action-oriented feedback, held the seriousness without demoralising, offered two concrete fixes, and closed with reinforcement and accountability. Now type or say aloud the follow-up message you would send Alex three weeks later.',
'Brilliant! Giving constructive feedback is one of the highest acts of professional care. You did not soften the truth to be kind — you delivered it with enough specificity and compassion to actually be useful. That is the difference between feedback that changes things and feedback that just makes you feel like you tried.')})

levels.append({'id':40,'journey_id':4,'journey_title':'Workplace Basics','title':'Handling Workplace Conflict',
'scenario':'You and a colleague, James, have had a growing tension over the past month. It started with a disagreement over a process decision, and since then small interactions have become strained. You sense James is avoiding you and there is a coolness in meetings. Left unaddressed, this will continue to affect the team. You decide to take the initiative.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You decide to approach James. How do you initiate the conversation?',
'"James — I\'ve been aware of some tension between us since the process disagreement and I\'d really like to address it directly. Could we find fifteen minutes this week?"',
['Wait for James to approach you first, since you did nothing wrong.','Raise it in the next team meeting: "I want to address something between James and me."','Ask a mutual colleague to mediate between you before you\'ve spoken directly.'],
'Taking the initiative, naming the tension directly but calmly, and requesting private time to talk is the most direct and respectful path to resolution.',
'Waiting for James to come to you when you have also noticed the tension is passive and allows it to continue. Raising it in a team meeting is publicly exposing a private conflict. Going to a mediator before speaking directly skips the most important step.'),
mcq(2,'James agrees to meet. In the meeting, you open. What do you say?',
'"I wanted to start by saying I think the process discussion got a bit heated and I\'m not sure we resolved it in a way that felt good to either of us. I\'m not here to relitigate it — I just want to make sure things are okay between us."',
['"James, I feel like you\'ve been avoiding me and it\'s affecting the whole team."','"I want to clear the air because this situation is making the team environment uncomfortable for me."','"I think we may have both said some things we didn\'t mean in that meeting.'],
'Opening with shared ownership of an unresolved tension — "I\'m not sure it felt good to either of us" — without accusation or immediate problem-solving invites James into a collaborative conversation.',
'"You\'ve been avoiding me and it\'s affecting the whole team" makes James the problem and the team the victim — both of which put him on the defensive. "This situation is making me uncomfortable" makes it about your experience without inviting James\'s. Suggesting you both said things you didn\'t mean may not be accurate and could feel presumptuous.'),
mcq(3,'James says, "Honestly — I felt dismissed in that meeting. I brought up a concern and it felt like it was brushed aside." How do you respond?',
'"I\'m sorry if it came across that way — that wasn\'t my intention but I can see how it might have felt dismissive. Can you tell me more about what specifically landed that way?"',
['"I didn\'t dismiss you — I just disagreed with your point."','"I felt dismissed too, actually — so we were both feeling it."','Say nothing and let an awkward silence develop while you decide what to think.'],
'Acknowledging the impact — "I can see how it might have felt" — without fully accepting the characterisation, while inviting more detail, keeps the conversation open and shows genuine interest in understanding.',
'"I didn\'t dismiss you, I just disagreed" is technically possible but lands as a denial of his experience. Immediately pivoting to your own feeling of being dismissed can feel like a competition of grievances. Silence at a moment of disclosure is cold and creates awkwardness.'),
mcq(4,'James explains what specifically felt dismissive. You can see he has a point. How do you close this part of the conversation?',
'"Thank you for explaining that — I understand what you mean now. I think I moved on too quickly without really hearing your concern out. I\'d like to do that now if you\'re open to it — what was the core of what you were trying to raise?"',
['"Okay — I get it. Sorry. Moving on."','Say, "I appreciate that, but to be fair, my view was also valid."','Offer a long, detailed explanation of your original decision to justify it.'],
'Thanking him for explaining, acknowledging your specific behaviour — "moved on too quickly" — and turning it into an invitation to actually hear the concern is the most restorative thing you can do.',
'"Sorry, moving on" is an acknowledgment without real repair. "My view was also valid" may be true but at this moment it reads as defensiveness. Justifying the original decision re-opens the argument when you came to address the relationship.'),
mcq(5,'The meeting ends positively. James says, "I\'m glad we talked — it was overdue." How do you close and follow up?',
'"Me too. Let\'s not let it get that long again — if either of us feels something, let\'s just say it. I\'ll send you a quick summary of the original concern you raised so we can actually look at it properly."',
['Say, "Great — let\'s forget it ever happened."','Do nothing afterward — the conversation resolved it.','Tell your manager the conflict with James is now resolved.'],
'Proposing a norm for future friction — "let\'s just say it" — and following through on the substantive issue that started it all demonstrates that you genuinely want to resolve both the relationship and the original problem.',
'"Let\'s forget it ever happened" erases the learning from the conversation. Doing nothing means there is no structural change to prevent a recurrence. Telling your manager before James has a chance to do so — or before it is fully resolved — can feel like a manoeuvre rather than transparency.')],
'response':resp('You took the initiative to address a workplace conflict, opened without accusation, heard James\'s experience with genuine curiosity, acknowledged your specific behaviour, and followed through on both the relationship and the underlying issue. Now type or say aloud the summary of James\'s original concern that you promised to send him.',
'Excellent! Handling workplace conflict requires courage, self-awareness, and genuine care for the relationship. You showed all three. The willingness to be the one who starts the conversation is one of the most important professional qualities there is.')})
